  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "DSH-PDR",
      "text": "**Plugin Detection and Response for DeepSeek Harness.**\n\n[](LICENSE)\n[](package.json)\n[](package.json)\n[](https://github.com/kipozannaki/DSH-PDR-Plugin/actions/workflows/ci.yml)\n\n**English** · [中文](./README.zh.md)\n\n---\n\nEvery plugin you install runs with the same privileges as the host: it can read your credentials,\nopen sockets, and spawn processes. Reviewing its source answers *what it could do* — and only\nuntil the next update."
    },
    {
      "section": "DSH-PDR",
      "text": "DSH-PDR answers a different question: **what did it actually do?**\n\nWhen the host is started through its launcher, it observes the running process, attributes the\n`fs` / network / subprocess calls plugins make to the plugin that made them, judges those calls\nagainst what the plugin claims to need, and shows you the result. Everything stays on your\nmachine. Attribution is best-effort, not a guarantee — see [Limitations](#limitations)."
    },
    {
      "section": "DSH-PDR",
      "text": "- 🔍 **Pre-install health card** — static capability surface, red-line combinations, PASS / WARN / BLOCK\n- 👁 **Runtime firewall** — per-plugin timeline of filesystem, network and subprocess activity\n- ⚖️ **Boundary judge** — declared contract × path class × behaviour baseline, graded normal / observe / suspicious / boundary\n- 📊 **In-app monitor tab** — a `行为监控` tab in the web UI's view bar: per-plugin call stats, red-line tags and a live timeline (auto-refresh), fed by the plugin's own in-host API\n- 🛑 **Block mode** (opt-in) — stops a declared-violating call before it executes; `credential-exfil` catches a credential read followed by egress in the same breath\n- 🔄 **Update guard** — capability / red-line / dependency diff, plus a trial install in an isolated child process (temp `DSH_HOME`, hardened timeout — a separate process, not a sandbox)\n- 📄 **Audit export** — JSON, Markdown, SARIF 2.1.0, CSV; generated offline from the event log, no host required\n- 🔔 **Webhook alerts** (off by default) — red-line events only, deduplicated and rate-limited; supports `json`, Slack and DingTalk payload formats\n- 📊 **Multi-source aggregation** — merge event logs from several profiles or machines, w"
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "```bash"
    },
    {
      "section": "1. install into a profile",
      "text": "dsh plugin --profile <profile> add file:/path/to/dsh-pdr-plugin"
    },
    {
      "section": "or, directly from this repository:",
      "text": "dsh plugin --profile <profile> add github:kipozannaki/DSH-PDR-Plugin"
    },
    {
      "section": "2. start the host through the launcher (not `dsh` directly — see below)",
      "text": "dsh-pdr --profile <profile> web"
    },
    {
      "section": "3. confirm the preload is in place",
      "text": "dsh-pdr --pdr-check\n```\n\nRequirements: **Node ≥ 22.12** (for `require(esm)`), **DSH ≥ 0.1.7**, **pnpm** (used by\n`dsh plugin add`).\nbash\nnode src/client/serve.js        # http://127.0.0.1:3123/firewall/\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "What it does",
      "text": "| Stage | Capability | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Before install | Health card: capability surface, red-line combinations, PASS / WARN / BLOCK | `src/inspector/` |\n| Before install | Trust score: weighted, auditable formula (red lines −30 each, credential access −5, network −3, subprocess −2) → bands 良好 / 注意 / 谨慎 / 高危 | `src/inspector/trust-score.js` |\n| Runtime | Behaviour probe: network / fs / subprocess, attributed per plugin | `src/probe/` |\n| Runtime | Red-line alerts: credentials+network, credentials+subprocess, cleartext egress | `src/probe/` |\n| Runtime | Boundary judge: contract × path class × baseline → normal / observe / suspicious / boundary | `src/judge/` |\n| Runtime | Contract inference: observe a cold start → draft → human confirmation; an inference never launders an observed red line | `src/judge/infer.js` |\n| Runtime | Block mode: pre-execution check, violation stopped in place (opt-in; `alarm` is the default) | `src/guard/` |\n| On change | Snapshot & rollback of the profile manifest trio (`package.json`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `cordis.patch.yml`) | `src/store/snapshot.js` |\n| On change | Update guard: capability diff + trial install in an isolated child process | `src/upd"
    },
    {
      "section": "Screenshot",
      "text": "```bash\nnode src/client/serve.js                     # demo data\nnode src/client/serve.js --jsonl <path>      # replay a real probe log ($DSH_HOME/firewall/events.jsonl)\n```\n\nPlain `index.html` + `app.js` + `style.css` — no framework, no build step. The standalone server and\nthe in-host mount share one route table (`src/client/api-handlers.mjs`), so the preview behaves\nexactly like the mounted panel."
    },
    {
      "section": "How it works",
      "text": "```\nconfig materialises ── earlyInstall expression installs the probe\n        │\npreload ────────────── dsh-pdr sets NODE_OPTIONS=--require …/preload.cjs  ← beats ESM facades\n        │"
    },
    {
      "section": "How it works",
      "text": "The probe wraps `RegistryService.prototype.plugin` before any plugin is instantiated, so plugin\nentry points run inside an `AsyncLocalStorage` context and the calls they start are attributed —\nincluding calls made through injected host services. This is the cheap path: measured on a real\nsession (~5 600 hooks), stack-based attribution costs ≈ 1.9 % of wall clock against ALS's\n≈ 0.05 % (per-op: 114 µs vs 2.9 µs), so `als` is the default. What `als` does not cover —\ncalls that never cross a wrapped entry point, the named-import gaps listed under\n[Limitations](#limitations) — falls back to `host` rather than being silently misattributed."
    },
    {
      "section": "How it works",
      "text": "Full architecture, hook-coverage matrix and the honest list of residual gaps:\n[`docs/DESIGN.md`](./docs/DESIGN.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Configuration",
      "text": "```yaml"
    },
    {
      "section": "the profile's cordis.patch.yml",
      "text": "- id: dsh-pdr-plugin\n  config:\n    dataDir: ~/.dsh/firewall     # default\n    probeMode: als               # als (default) | stack (slower) | both\n    maxBytes: 8388608            # rotate events.jsonl at 8 MB, keep 3\n    policy:\n      mode: alarm                # alarm (default) | block\n      rules:\n        credential-exfil: block\n    alerts:\n      enabled: false             # off: not one byte leaves the machine\n      url: https://your-webhook.example/hook\n      rules: []                  # empty = every red line\n      format: json               # json | slack | dingtalk\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Declaring a contract (optional, for plugin authors)",
      "text": "```jsonc\n// package.json\n\"firewall\": {\n  \"hosts\": [\"api.example.com\"],     // declared destinations; going outside → scope violation\n  \"grants\": [\"global-credentials\"]  // explicit authorisation for sensitive targets\n\nA contract is **declared freedom**: staying inside it is silent, leaving it is recorded. When\nnothing is declared, the contract is derived from the health card's capability surface."
    },
    {
      "section": "CLI",
      "text": "```bash\ndsh-pdr web                        # = dsh web, with the preload\ndsh-pdr headless \"run tests\"       # any dsh subcommand\ndsh-pdr --pdr-check                # print which preload is in use"
    },
    {
      "section": "offline export — no host, no hooks, just reads the JSONL",
      "text": "dsh-pdr audit --format md\ndsh-pdr audit --format sarif --out audit.sarif\ndsh-pdr audit --format csv   --out audit.csv\ndsh-pdr audit --format json  --out audit.json"
    },
    {
      "section": "multi-source aggregation (several profiles / machines / archives)",
      "text": "dsh-pdr aggregate --format md\ndsh-pdr aggregate --source prod=/m1/.dsh/firewall --source dev=/m2/.dsh/firewall\n```\n\n`aggregate` reads rotated files too (`events.jsonl.1/.2/.3`), de-duplicates across sources and\nreports the counts, and counts unparsable lines instead of dropping them silently. Measured on 4\nfiles / 127 000 events: 1 252 duplicates found."
    },
    {
      "section": "API",
      "text": "Other plugins can inject the `firewall` service:\n\n```js\nconst api = ctx.firewall;\n\napi.getEvents({ actor: 'some-plugin', flag: 'credentials-access', limit: 100 });\napi.getRedFlags();                 // red-line alerts"
    },
    {
      "section": "Privacy",
      "text": "- Events, snapshots, contracts and health cards live in `$DSH_HOME/firewall/`. **No telemetry.**\n- The only outbound path is a webhook **you** configure. It is off by default, sends red-line\n  events only — never the full timeline, never file contents, never credentials. The audit\n  report records only the webhook's origin (scheme + host), never the URL with any token in it.\n- The plugin holds itself to its own standard: no credential access, no silent network. Its own\n  capability surface, as scanned by its own health card, is part of the test suite."
    },
    {
      "section": "Limitations",
      "text": "Stated plainly, because a security tool that overstates its coverage is worse than none:"
    },
    {
      "section": "Limitations",
      "text": "- **Heuristic, not a proof.** A static scan cannot see credentials injected at runtime; that is\n  what the runtime layer is for. On 154 real ecosystem plugins the static pass recalls 66.1 % at\n  17.2 % false positives. Being listed on a plugin marketplace is not a security review, and\n  neither is this README: treat every plugin, including this one, as untrusted until observed.\n- **Not an adversarial sandbox.** A plugin that deliberately targets the instrumentation\n  (native modules, `process.binding`, patching the probe) can evade it. The goal is continuous,\n  attributable visibility.\n- **Attribution needs the launcher.** Without `dsh-pdr` (or another host-level preload), the\n  in-plugin probe installs too late and attribution collapses — measured **0 of 3** on a real\n  session with everything falling back to `host`. The DSH **Desktop app cannot be launched\n  through the launcher**, so its sessions are currently outside attribution; the health card and\n  the offline `audit` / `aggregate` commands are unaffected. A host-level `--import` hook is the\n  tracked next step.\n- **Known gaps** — named imports of `child_process` / `http` / `net` when the facade predates the\n  probe. `fs` is"
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "```bash\nnpm test              # all 22 suites (PASS / SKIP / FAIL; any failure exits 1)\nnpm run test:list     # what would run\nnode test/smoke.mjs   # one suite\n```\n\nSuites that need a real environment (a real plugin, a profile, an external baseline) print\n`SKIP: <reason>` and how to restore them, and exit 0. They never pretend to pass. Any failing\nassertion exits 1.\n\nWhat each suite covers: [`docs/TESTING.md`](./docs/TESTING.md)."
    },
    {
      "section": "Updating an installed copy",
      "text": "`file:` dependencies are **copied** into the profile, not symlinked — changing code or bumping the\nversion does not reach it, and `add` answers \"Already up to date\". Remove then add:\n\n```bash\ndsh plugin --profile <p> remove dsh-pdr-plugin\ndsh plugin --profile <p> add file:/path/to/dsh-pdr-plugin\n```"
    },
    {
      "section": "Documentation",
      "text": "| Document | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| [`docs/DESIGN.md`](./docs/DESIGN.md) | architecture, attribution, hook coverage, residual gaps |\n| [`docs/TESTING.md`](./docs/TESTING.md) | every suite, what it asserts, environment variables |\n| [`docs/LESSONS.md`](./docs/LESSONS.md) | findings that changed the implementation |\n| [`CHANGELOG.md`](./CHANGELOG.md) | what changed, when |"
    },
    {
      "section": "Listing metadata",
      "text": "For plugin marketplaces: screenshots are declared in\n[`screenshots.json`](./screenshots.json) and shipped in `assets/`. The package publishes no\n`postinstall` / `prepare` script, so a source install never needs a pnpm build approval."
    },
    {
      "section": "License",
      "text": "MIT — see [LICENSE](./LICENSE)."
    }
